## 0.6.41 — Item Grant matcher was checking a `type` value that doesn't exist, plus a data-loss scare

Live-testing 0.6.40 immediately surfaced two problems, reported by the user mid-session as sharp
frustration ("ALL it does is RAISE the REACH... why can you NOT get it to just flick a flag").

**The real bug**: Quartermaster's Due's matcher (`item-grants.mjs`, added in 0.6.39) required
`system.type === "Main-Hand"` or `"Off-Hand"` exactly. Querying the live compendium and Caelon
Voss's actual owned items showed `type` is free descriptive text an item's author just types in —
"Ranged, 1h", "Foci, 1h", "Foci, 2h", "Off-Hand", "Medium", "Passive" (Toolkits/Kits) — there is no
"Main-Hand" value anywhere in real content, and never has been. The 0.6.39 matcher was written from
the rulebook's own wording ("Main-Hand, Off-Hand, or Armor item") without checking what the actual
stored data looks like, so it excluded nearly everything a real character owns. Fixed by matching
on `category` instead (weapon/armor outright; `tool` MINUS `excludeTypes: ["Passive"]`, since a
held Focus/talisman/gauntlet is filed under category "tool" alongside Toolkits in this data, and
"Passsive" is the one type value that reliably marks an item as a Toolkit/Kit rather than
worn/wielded gear). Verified live afterward: the picker now correctly lists Power Focus, Sidearm,
and Reinforced Armor (Caelon Voss's actual Main-Hand/Off-Hand/Armor items) instead of nothing.

**Separately, a real data-loss incident**: while testing, "Reinforced Armor" — a pre-existing item
on Caelon Voss with no connection to this session's work — was found completely missing from the
actor's `_source` data (confirmed via direct inspection, not a rendering artifact) between one test
and the next, with no corresponding delete call anywhere in the code that touched it. The most
likely cause: this session force-stopped the running Foundry process via `taskkill`/
`Stop-Process` several times in a row (for compendium pack rebuilds) rather than Foundry's own
graceful shutdown, and a subsequent reload landed on the Setup screen showing "Backup In
Progress" — strongly suggesting Foundry runs its own periodic/triggered world-backup routine, and
a forceful stop mid-write (to the LevelDB actor store or during a backup snapshot) is a plausible
way to lose the most-recently-written document. Recreated the item manually via `actor.
createEmbeddedDocuments` using the values visible in an earlier screenshot (category armor,
Fortitude/Resilience +1, Reach 1) since no in-app undo/version history exists for embedded Items.
**Lesson for future sessions**: prefer Foundry's own graceful shutdown (in-app "Exit" from the
Game Master menu, or at minimum give it time to flush after a SIGTERM) over an immediate
`taskkill`/`Stop-Process`, especially right after a document write — the pack-rebuild workflow
this session used (stop → rebuild → relaunch) needs the "stop" step to actually be graceful, not
just fast, and this incident is exactly the failure mode to watch for if the same pattern recurs.

## 0.6.42 — Equipment sheet / Item Creation Wizard / Bulk Import UI overhaul

Live-testing 0.6.41's Reach fix put a freshly-created blank Equipment item sheet in front of the
user, and the reaction was immediate and pointed: inconsistent capitalization between fields,
Reach Exception exposition on every item regardless of relevance, Category and Kind as two
overlapping dropdowns describing one thing, Slot/Reach Bonus/Tags/Quantity shown unconditionally
even where they don't apply, and a request to restructure Category into a single Weapon/Armor/
Shield/Toolkit/Consumable Kit/etc. choice with Modular assembly as plain text for now. Mid-fix, a
second and third message widened scope to the rich-text editor fields ("can't type... it's all one
color") and then explicitly to the Item Creation Wizard and Bulk Import ("hot fucking trash"),
followed by a request for per-type Bulk Import templates instead of one combined Cards template.

**The Category/Kind merge and per-category field visibility** are described in full in
CHANGELOG.md's 0.6.42 entry. Two things worth recording here that aren't just "what shipped":

1. **Changing a StringField's `choices` list retroactively hides, not just resets, any embedded
   Item already using a removed value.** Narrowing `category`'s choices from
   weapon/armor/tool/gear to the new 7-value enum immediately made every existing "tool"/"guard"-
   categoried Equipment Item vanish from `actor.items` entirely (not merely reset to a default) —
   confirmed by checking `actor._source.items`, where the document was still fully intact. This
   is a real, previously-undocumented Foundry behavior for this project: a TypeDataModel field
   that fails `choices` validation causes the whole embedded Document to fail construction and get
   silently dropped from the runtime collection, with no error surfaced to the GM. The safe fix
   pattern (used here, and worth reusing for any future enum narrowing): temporarily widen
   `choices` to include the old deprecated value(s), reload so affected Items re-instantiate,
   migrate them via the normal `.update()` API now that they're reachable again, then remove the
   deprecated values and reload once more to confirm nothing is silently missing. Attempting the
   migration via `actor.updateEmbeddedDocuments()` directly by `_id` while an item is in this
   hidden state does NOT work either — Foundry's own update-handling code dereferences the
   (nonexistent) client-side Document instance and throws, so there's no way around actually
   re-widening the schema first.
   - Separately, this hunt turned up (and ruled out) a second, unrelated question: an earlier
     session (0.6.39) had a genuine actor Item ("Reinforced Armor") vanish with no code path that
     should have deleted it, at the time blamed on a forceful `taskkill` mid-write. That guess
     wasn't wrong exactly, but this session shows the *category-choices* mechanism is a second,
     independently-confirmed way an Item can disappear without deletion — worth keeping in mind
     before assuming corruption when an Item goes missing after a schema change, not just after a
     forceful process stop.
2. **Foundry core's blanket `button { height: var(--button-size); min-height: var(--button-size) }`
   (foundry2.css) silently wrecks any custom `flex-direction: column` icon+label button layout**
   unless overridden — the fixed single-line height crushes a stacked icon-over-text layout into
   overlapping the same few pixels. This bit both the ProseMirror editor fix (menu + content area
   fighting over a `--min-height` too small once a real toolbar renders — the actual root cause of
   "can't type in these fields," not just a color/contrast issue as first assumed) and the new
   Content Wizard/Bulk Import icon-card pickers (icon and label text drawn on top of each other on
   the first pass, fixed by giving `.content-type-card` an explicit `height: auto; min-height:
   72px` to override core's rule). Worth checking for on any future custom `<button>` layout in
   this system that isn't a single line of text.

**Bulk Import** also gained templates it never had (Chassis/Fitting/Augment) and lost a bad one
(the combined Action+Reaction "Combat Cards" template with a `kind` disambiguator column) — see
CHANGELOG.md. The three per-type create-or-update branches (previously near-duplicated: unlock
pack, load existing docs, loop-match-or-create, restore lock) collapsed into one generic loop
parameterized by each TEMPLATES entry's own `pack`/`type`/`img`/`toSystem`, which is what made
adding three more types (chassis/fitting/augment) a data-only change instead of three more copies
of the same loop.

**Verification**: `node --check` across the entire `module/`+`scripts/` tree and `{{#`/`{{/`
balance across the entire `templates/` tree (not just touched files) after every batch of edits,
plus live verification in Foundry after each reload — the equipment category migration, the
Chassis/Toolkit field-visibility rules, the ProseMirror typing fix (typed text and confirmed it
landed in the saved field, then cleared it), and both wizards' new icon grids were all confirmed
visually and functionally in a running client, not just read back from source.

**A user screenshot of a downloaded equipment CSV template**, sent unprompted mid-fix, made a
sharper version of the same underlying point explicit: Slot and Quantity were still columns in the
Bulk Import templates (and Quantity still an always-visible field on the Equipment sheet, having
only gated Slot behind `item.actor` in the first pass) even though neither is a property of what
an item fundamentally IS — they're properties of an owned COPY of that item, assigned once a
