### 2026-09-09 — 0.6.46 broke localization entirely: leaf/namespace key collisions

The user installed v0.6.46 fresh (new local Foundry instance, brand-new Actor — explicitly not a
caching artifact, and confirmed via a full app restart that still reproduced it) and every sheet
showed raw localization keys instead of text — "ESSENCE.Common.Tier" instead of "Tier", and
critically also the window title's `TYPES.Actor.character`, a key that predates this session
entirely and had never broken before. That second detail was the important clue: a single missing
*key* only breaks that one string (Foundry's `Localization#localize` falls back to returning the
raw key when a lookup misses) — but *every* key breaking, including old ones, means the language
file never loaded into `game.i18n` at all.

Verified the shipped release zip's `lang/en.json` directly (downloaded and parsed it) — valid,
532 entries, both flagged keys resolved correctly in isolation. Verified the user's actual local
Foundry install directory on this same machine
(`C:\Users\shane\AppData\Local\FoundryVTT\Data\systems\essence-system`) — also fine, matching the
release exactly, version `0.6.46` correctly installed. So the *file* was never the problem; the
question became why Foundry's own loader would reject a syntactically-valid JSON file.

Root cause, found by simulating Foundry's own loading behavior rather than just re-validating JSON
syntax: Foundry doesn't store `lang/en.json`'s keys as a flat dictionary — it expands each dotted
key into a nested object tree (`"A.B.C": "x"` becomes `translations.A.B.C = "x"`), the same
convention `TYPES.Actor.character` has always relied on. That expansion throws when a key is
*simultaneously* a leaf string value and a prefix namespace another key needs to nest under.
0.6.46 introduced exactly that shape 5 times: `"ESSENCE.Item.Equipment": "Equipment"` (used as the
Equipment tab label) sat right alongside 28 other keys like `"ESSENCE.Item.Equipment.Reach"` that
need `ESSENCE.Item.Equipment` to be an *object*, not a string — same collision for `Item.Species`
(14 conflicts), `Item.Heritage` (4), `Item.Distinction` (7), and `Settings.RoleBudgets.Field` (10,
the dynamic per-field labels in the Role Budgets settings app). The tree-expansion step throwing
mid-file aborts the *whole* file load, which is why unrelated, pre-existing keys broke too — a
single bad key doesn't fail gracefully in isolation, it takes the entire language pack down with
it. Wrote a small script (`keys.some(o => o !== k && o.startsWith(k + '.'))`) to exhaustively
detect every such collision across all 532 keys rather than trusting a manual re-scan, confirming
exactly these 5 and no others.

**Fix**: renamed the 5 colliding leaf keys to a non-colliding `*Label` suffix
(`ESSENCE.Item.EquipmentLabel`, `SpeciesLabel`, `HeritageLabel`, `DistinctionLabel`,
`Settings.RoleBudgets.FieldLabel`) and updated their 12 template call sites. Re-ran the full
validation suite from the 0.6.46 session (key-coverage diff, now also checking for zero remaining
structural collisions, brace-balance) before shipping. v0.6.47, see CHANGELOG.md.

**New standing lesson worth remembering for any future localization work in this system**: a
Foundry `lang/*.json` key must never be both a leaf and a prefix of another key. When adding a
"type label" key alongside a namespace of related keys for that same type (e.g. wanting both "the
word Equipment" and "Equipment.Reach", "Equipment.Armory", etc.), the bare/shortest name needs its
own distinguishing suffix (`*Label`, `*Name`) — it can never be the literal shared prefix. Given
how catastrophically this fails (whole-file load abort, not a graceful single-key fallback), a
"check for collisions" step is worth adding to any future bulk key-authoring pass before shipping,
not just JSON-syntax validation.

